<section class="card">
  <h2 class="card-title">loaded policy</h2>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p class="cell-dim">What the daemon has loaded and attached, zone by zone — not the policy source on disk. A <span class="mono">.fw</span> file that was edited and never reloaded reads exactly like one that is live, so nothing on this page comes from a file.</p>
    <div id="policy-table">
      {% include "fw/policy_table" %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2 class="card-title">policy-wide</h2>
  <div class="card-body">
    <table class="data-table">
      <tbody>
{% for f in features %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ f.label }}</td>
          <td class="cell-{{ f.semantic }}">{{ f.value }}</td>
        </tr>
{% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <h2 class="card-title">rules — in policy order, per zone</h2>
  <div class="card-body">
    <p class="cell-dim">The rules of the policy that is LOADED. The compiler wrote them into the bundle manifest and the load that put these programs in the packet path captured them beside the objects — so they belong to the policy that is running, not to whatever is in the bundle directory now. Nothing on this page reads a file.</p>
    <p class="cell-dim">There is no position column and no hit count: the numbers <span class="mono">no rule</span> takes belong to the source statements, which are numbered differently, and pairing a rule with a counter by position is how the removed rule surface put every number beside the wrong rule. The counters a policy declares are on <a href="/counters">/counters</a>, under their own names.</p>
    {% include "fw/policy_rules" %}
  </div>
</section>
